1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,538
This is revision B of our magnetic cooling head.

2
00:00:03,538 --> 00:00:13,538
Unlike the earlier two part layout, this assembly includes the actual magnet volumes, their dry wells, a locator, a steel return yoke, and supporting spacers.

3
00:00:13,538 --> 00:00:16,513
The exploded view shows how these parts relate.

4
00:00:16,513 --> 00:00:21,767
Display colors distinguish components, not necessarily their real surface finishes.

5
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:26,263
The copper body now has seven blind magnet wells.

6
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:32,882
Water occupies the pocket around the solid copper islands; the magnets enter the dry holes inside those islands.

7
00:00:32,882 --> 00:00:35,128
The wells stop before the target face.

8
00:00:35,128 --> 00:00:43,167
We increased the body diameter to eighty two millimetres to accommodate the wells, seal glands, coolant passages, and fastening pattern.

9
00:00:44,767 --> 00:00:50,428
Six eight by twenty millimetre rod magnets surround two stacked twelve by ten millimetre discs.

10
00:00:50,428 --> 00:00:56,864
In this proposed orientation, the outer poles face north toward the target and the center stack faces south.

11
00:00:56,864 --> 00:00:59,307
The steel plate provides the return path.

12
00:00:59,307 --> 00:01:01,810
A plastic locator guides the magnet bases.

13
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:07,233
This arrangement fits mechanically; its field strength and racetrack are not yet validated.

14
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:13,342
Each dry well needs its own face O ring at the body and cover interface.

15
00:01:13,342 --> 00:01:16,034
A perimeter ring closes the outer boundary.

16
00:01:16,034 --> 00:01:23,360
That makes eight primary face seals: six around the outer wells, one around the center, and one around the perimeter.

17
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:25,739
The green traces mark their locations.

18
00:01:25,739 --> 00:01:29,433
They are explanatory graphics, not seal compression models.

19
00:01:31,033 --> 00:01:34,407
First the cover approaches and closes the cooling body.

20
00:01:34,407 --> 00:01:37,473
Its seven dry passages align with the blind wells.

21
00:01:37,473 --> 00:01:40,724
Next, the yoke and magnet pack advance from the rear.

22
00:01:40,724 --> 00:01:45,324
The rods and center stack pass through the cover into their matching wells.

23
00:01:45,324 --> 00:01:47,777
The yoke seats on three support spacers.

24
00:01:47,777 --> 00:01:54,033
This animation shows the nominal assembly geometry; fasteners and seals still require final detailing.

25
00:01:55,633 --> 00:01:58,013
This cutaway reveals the fitted magnets.

26
00:01:58,013 --> 00:02:05,686
Their tips sit four millimetres behind the target seating face, including a one point five millimetre gap to the blind well ends.

27
00:02:05,686 --> 00:02:09,672
Target thickness adds to the distance to the plasma facing surface.

28
00:02:09,672 --> 00:02:15,442
The spacers carry the structural clamping load, so the magnets are not used as compression posts.

29
00:02:15,442 --> 00:02:17,167
Clearances shown are nominal.

30
00:02:18,767 --> 00:02:23,401
With the cover and magnets hidden, the blue floor reveals the coolant pocket.

31
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:29,600
Water enters through one relocated port, divides around the dry islands, and returns through the other.

32
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:33,513
The moving dots show two possible routes, not a fluid simulation.

33
00:02:33,513 --> 00:02:38,147
The copper divider discourages a direct shortcut, but bypass must be checked.

34
00:02:38,147 --> 00:02:42,300
The ports and rear utility routes lie between outer magnet positions.

35
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:48,454
The assembled head now reserves space for the magnets and utilities together.

36
00:02:48,454 --> 00:02:55,077
Fifteen candidate part instances pass nominal collision checks, and a separate fitting volume check also passes.

37
00:02:55,077 --> 00:02:58,507
That establishes geometric fit, not operating performance.

38
00:02:58,507 --> 00:03:09,033
We still need magnetic field analysis, seal and pressure validation, cooling assessment, and the complete vacuum housing and electrical connection to our selected RF feedthrough.
